**Findings**

1. Line 10: `replace_realdata("abc", "x")`
The absent `realData: {` marker becomes `start == -1`, and the function returns corrupted text like `ab{xbc` instead of failing closed.

2. Line 15: `replace_realdata("pre realData: { a: '} still string', b: 1 } post", " z: 2 }")`
The brace counter treats `}` inside a quoted JS string as structural, so it cuts the object early and corrupts the output HTML.

3. Line 10: `replace_realdata("<!-- realData: {} -->\n<script>const app = { realData: { old: 1 } };</script>", " new: 1 }")`
The selector can be satisfied by a comment before the live binding, so the comment is rewritten while the actual data remains unchanged.

4. Lines 94-116 and 122-143: target file input `<title>WRONG</title><script>app = { realData: {} };</script>`
All scaffold `text.replace(...)` calls can be no-ops, but the script still writes the file and prints a size, so the wrapper reports success while title/protocol/include metadata remain wrong.

No finding proven for CRLF/LF text comparison, merged-cell/off-by-one tabulation, zero conflated with absent, or negation dropped.

